(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608318100-agent-host-skill-and-plugin-in-kotoba-lang")
  (:adr/title "A skill and a plugin repo for the agent hosts, and the plugin- role that names the second")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-31")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608318000-hermes-residents-join-the-bots-plane"
                "adr-2608197300-one-authorization-decider"
                "adr-2608650000-two-kinds-of-cannot"
                "adr-2607299000-repository-rules-move-to-the-superproject"))
  (:adr/body "# The agent hosts get a skill and a plugin of their own\n\nThe Hermes residents (40 of them, ADR-2608318000) reach this workspace by\nrunning nbb scripts inside a git worktree and reading what comes back. That is\nthe whole interface. There are two things they cannot do with it, and both are\nthings the agent gets wrong from memory instead:\n\n1. **Ask whether a Kotoba refusal is permanent.** A safety invariant and an\n   unfinished backend arrive as the same message. Only `:disposition` in\n   `lang/surface-status.edn` separates them.\n2. **Ask which backends have qualified a capability.** Every readiness value\n   in this workspace that was copied into prose went stale, including in the\n   repo-wide CLAUDE.md more than once -- which is why CLAUDE.md now carries\n   the *reader* for the kit files and refuses to carry their contents.\n\nBoth are read-only questions with an authoritative answer sitting in a file.\nNeither was reachable as a tool.\n\n## Two repos, two roles\n\n**`kotoba-lang/skill-guest-authoring`** -- role `:agent-instruction-package`\n(`skill-` in `manifest/repository-rules.edn`). A plain `SKILL.md`, which is\nthe same format both agent hosts here read, so one file serves Claude Code\nand Hermes without a translation layer. It states the procedure and NEVER a\nreadiness value: a skill that quotes a qualification table is a skill that\nwill one day be confidently wrong. Where the answer is a measurement, it\npoints at the measurement.\n\nThis is the second instance of the `skill-` role anywhere in kotoba-lang;\n`skill-loop-system-dynamics` was the first.\n\n**`kotoba-lang/plugin-hermes`** -- role `:agent-host-plugin-adapter`, a\nprefix this ADR adds. Three read-only tools (`kotoba_surface_status`,\n`kotoba_capability_kits`, `kotoba_check`) in a Hermes backend plugin.\n\n## Why `plugin-` is a new prefix and not a stretched `action-`\n\n`action-` adapts this workspace's work to GitHub's runner. `plugin-` adapts\nit to an agent host that loads code and calls it as tools. Same shape,\ndifferent runner -- so the same `:must-not` list applies, and the new rule\ncopies it: no domain contract, no second copy of a decision that already has\nan owner.\n\nThe host dictates the language, which is exactly why the decision must not\nlive in it. `read_authority.cljs` holds every judgement about what an\nauthority file means, beside the data and in the same language; `tools.py`\nturns arguments into an argv and an exit code into a protocol answer. A\nPython EDN reader would be a second decider -- two answers that agree until\nthe day they do not, with no test that would notice. This is the shape\n`scripts/hermes-dashboard-auth-did` established here (ADR-2608197300 sections\n3 and 5), recorded in the rule as `:prior-art` because it predates the prefix\nand still lives in the superproject rather than a repo.\n\n## The three-valued exit code is in the rule, not just the code\n\n`:distinguish-could-not-answer-from-answered-no` is a `:must` for this role.\n\n    0  answered              -> tool_result\n    1  answered: not present -> tool_result, found=false\n    2  could not answer      -> tool_error\n\n\"this entry is not in the authority\" is a measurement. \"I could not open the\nauthority\" is not. Collapsed, an unread file reports as an absent feature,\nwhich is the failure class ADR-2608136000 names and the one an agent is least\nable to notice: it asked, it got a clean answer, and the answer was fiction.\n\nThe answer also carries the authority's own `as-of`. Measured while writing\nthis: the local checkout answered `2026-08-15` where `origin/main` said\n`2026-08-30`. The tool reporting the date is what makes a stale checkout\nvisible as a stale checkout instead of as a language that stopped moving.\n\n`:ship-a-probe-that-verifies-an-install` is the other `:must`. The plugin's\n`probe.py` runs twelve checks in three pairs, each direction of each\ndistinction; changing the absent-workspace exit from 2 to 1 turns two of them\nred, which is how the pairing was verified rather than asserted.\n\n## Not done\n\nBoth repos are read-only. Nothing in them compiles, lands a change, or\nadvances a pin, and `kotoba_check` says plainly that a passing `amu check` is\nnot a run -- measured, a wrong type for `document-bool` passes check and fails\nat the value phase. Making a proposal is a governed act and belongs to the\nbot's own verifier, not to a tool the model can call.\n\n## Where these are installed, and what drifts\n\nBoth are installed on the operator workstation, and an installed copy is a\nCOPY -- the Hermes convention here (`hermes-hyakka-bots` README) is that the\nrepo is the reviewable original and the install drifts. Re-copy after editing;\ndo not edit the install.\n\n| what | installed at |\n|---|---|\n| the plugin | `~/.hermes/hermes-agent/plugins/kotoba/` |\n| the skill | `~/.hermes/skills/software-development/guest-authoring/` |\n\nVerified at install, not assumed: the plugin loads in Hermes's own interpreter,\nregisters three tools into the `kotoba` toolset, `available()` is true with a\nworkspace and false without one, and a live call returns a real answer.\n`probe.py` passes 12/12.\n\n**The gateway's launchd job was edited.** `KOTOBA_WORKSPACE` was added to\n`~/Library/LaunchAgents/ai.hermes.gateway.plist`'s `EnvironmentVariables` and\nthe job reloaded (`bootout` + `bootstrap` -- `kickstart -k` restarts the\nprocess but reuses the cached job definition, so the new variable did not\nappear until the job was re-bootstrapped). Without it the tools register and\nrefuse to dispatch, which is the designed unconfigured behaviour, not a\nfailure.\n\nThe other three gateways (`-codinator`, `-hyakka-corpus`, `-tabi-mikake`) were\nNOT edited. Their profiles do not carry `KOTOBA_WORKSPACE`, so the tools are\ninert there until an operator adds it the same way.\n\n`dashboard_auth/did`, cited above as prior art, is **not** installed -- the\nlive Hermes carries only `basic`, `drain`, `nous` and `self_hosted`. It is\nprior art for the shape, not a running component.")))
